            <history xml:id="history_id47a22c5c">
               <p xml:id="idm38">See introduction to SchW B5:Coll.002.<lb xml:id="idm39"/> Song is on pp. 12–13 (print). Scheibe's foreword in the printed edition mentions that 'in the eighth song, "Die Freyheit", I have imitated the nightingale as it warbles here in the Rosenborg Gardens and finally written it down most correctly. One should know that – due to its ornamentations and curling singing – this so famous and pleasant bird already in Jutland and the so-called region “Harde Wendsyssel” looses most of its colourful song. The further north it goes, it retains but a sheer beautiful, pure and hollow warble that is not without grace and still charming' (p. 7). Scheibe continues explaining the melodic structure and ornaments of the piece. The Rosenborg Gardens was well-known for its nightingales. The allegorical aspects of the song were later criticised in a review published in<rend xml:id="idm40" fontstyle="italic"> Hamburgische Unterhaltungen</rend> questioning whether Scheibe had reproduced the nightingale’s melody correctly. Scheibe responds in his second part of the collection (introduction, p. 2; SchW B5:Coll.003): 'Meanwhile, in order to confirm the provided description of the nightingale’s song, I have to say that during the last two summers I have become even more convinced of the correctness of that description and will only add that occasionally one may hear a nightingale sing several rather individual, but pure and full notes with or without trills, and thus still without the modulation that I have written down.'</p>
            </history>
